{
  "dom_id": "step-t5394",
  "name": "Introduction",
  "problem": "Given a sphere with radius $r= 3\\:\\textrm{cm},$ how do we calculate its volume?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234518/assets/8df0347cb30ac58b9cbcef4b7a7e25dfd35c899b1c833cdf2e8e22bcfc51971f.png)\n\nIn general, we can find the volume of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV= \\dfrac 4 3 \\pi r^3,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting $r=3\\:\\textrm{cm}$ into the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac 4 3 \\pi \\cdot (3)^3\n=36 \\pi \\:\\textrm{cm}^3.\n$$\n\nWe often express the volume of a sphere as a multiple of $\\pi.$ However, we can also use a calculator to get a numerical approximation. In this case, we get\n\n$$\nV \\approx 113.10 \\:\\textrm{cm}^3\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places.",
